I'm pleased to welcome back to the  program David McAfee who's a Religious  Studies graduate a six-time published  author founder of the party of Reason  and progress and the new book is called  no sacred cows which deals with really a  lot of subject matter that we talk about  on the program so so first David you  have been studying and researching  everything from sects to cults to the  belief in the paranormal and religious  beliefs for a very long time so what  what made you want to look more  specifically at what you talked about in  this particular book I started studying  religion specifically when I was 18 I  started majoring in religious studies at  UCSB but it was a few years later that I  started realizing that there was this  gap in information about other  supernatural beliefs and people who  separated them so much and I just saw  any similarities between say  Christianity and it's supernatural  practices like prayer and then other  non-religious supernatural aspects and I  just was so curious to find more  connections between those two and so  that's what I set out to do yeah and  there's a number of studies which point  out I believe accurately that there is a  decrease in sort of devout religious  belief and also just more casual  association with organized religion but  at the same time you talk in the book  about how we're not seeing the same an  equivalent I guess we would say decrease  in belief in supernatural claims that  are outside of religious belief so first  how do we measure that and then we can  maybe get into some of the more common  beliefs yeah and that's exactly accurate  every study that I've seen every  reputable study that I've seen has shown  a rise in people who don't identify with  any particular religious tradition they  call it the rise of the nuns the the  unaffiliated people and so a lot of  people assume that that means that we  are seeing a lot more critical thinking  and people are thinking through these  things and deciding not to adhere to  these traditions but when you look at  other polls about belief in ghosts and  psychics and and  supernatural beliefs that you would  think would be on the same tier they  tend to be rising at a pretty rapid rate  and so that's why I wrote this book was  because I thought that that needed the  attention because people seem to be  under the impression that they're always  correlated and it's really not true what  would be a top-line general explanation  because on the one hand we like to think  that the reason for the decline in  religiosity is as science develops and  as sort of knowledge of empiricism  spreads through the world  it maybe eliminates the need or place  for organized or devout religious  beliefs but if that were the sole reason  or primary reason you'd imagine you'd  have fewer people believing in psychics  and ancient aliens as well exactly I  think the problem is that people aren't  necessarily thinking more critically  about all their beliefs they might be  pushing a religion away for any number  of reasons for one it's it's more  ancient than a lot of these beliefs it  ties into a specific dogma so with  ghosts and and other supernatural  beliefs there's not really this book  this bounds you to believe in it and it  might be a push away from that away from  the rigidity of religion and toward the  fluidity of New Age beliefs and what are  the most common I mean you talked about  psychics and you alluded to some others  but when we're talking about  supernatural beliefs what's that what's  included there and what are the most  common I would say angels and demons  tend to be the most common when I looked  at polls it seemed like even more people  believed in angels in the United States  than identified with Christianity from  which you would think that they would be  getting the angel concept but there's an  entire separate belief system there from  people who believe in angels but don't  believe in any religion they believe in  guardian angels or just some big concept  that they grew up with but it's true  with all of these different supernatural  beliefs we can name thousands of them  and and I do in the book but I think the  problem is that people are just wanting  to believe in something bigger and  they're moving away from religion they  need something else too  move toward that's not always something  supernatural but in many cases it is in  other cases it's conspiracy theories  which also factor in that same idea that  there's something greater controlling  everything well that was what I was next  going to sort of mention which is the  overlap in the sort of processes that  take place in someone's mind that get  them to the conclusion of some  supernatural belief or concluding that a  conspiracy theory is actually the  explanation for some phenomena in our  world rather than whatever the so-called  official story is do you believe that it  is the same sort of process that gets  people to those end conclusions  mm-hm I much argue that in the book that  that the same failing the same failures  of critical thinking lead to all of  these different false beliefs whether  it's religion or even these Colts or  conspiracy theories or other  supernatural beliefs it's because  somebody didn't take the time to think  okay let's look at what has been  scientifically proven and then we  compare that to what what I want to  believe you got to separate your desires  you have to separate what your friends  and family have told you to believe  these are all influences that aren't  necessarily going to get you to the  right place but people trust their  family they trust their friends and so  if they're if they say that they saw a  ghost then you had a feeling ones that  you might have seen a ghost that belief  becomes solidified and there's not  really much that can change it it's  similar to religion in that instance  talk a little bit about both for this  book but also in your past you've done  some of this some of what I guess I'd  call them undercover incidents or  undercover attempts to learn more about  some of these phenomena definitely this  book was kind of my biggest step in that  direction because it contains I think at  least 10 different interviews with  believers with psychics with members of  the Westboro Baptist Church with the  great-grandson of l ron Hubbard and and  not all of these interviews not in all  of them do I identify myself as a  skeptic I just basically asking  questions to these peoples  and in this book I decided to take it  one step further and I actually trained  myself to become a psychic to become a  seer a cold reader and I started  performing on Venice Beach and I went  undercover in that way and it really  really opened my eyes to how these  beliefs are formed and solidified  because there were people who were so  convinced by my presentation which was  completely not magic of course that they  that they wouldn't believe me when I  revealed at the end that I was a skeptic  and that this was a thought experiment  they refused to believe me they said  that I was just trying to cover myself  so that the government wouldn't come get  me I I had people trying to give me so  much money and I was like no these are  completely free readings I didn't take a  dollar but the this one woman in  particular just wanted to give me money  and I was like this must be such an easy  job for the people who really do it and  want to take money so I mean I think  that how long it takes one to learn the  skill of cold reading which is sort of  the underlying skill in what is  colloquially described as being a  psychic I think that depending on one's  emotional intelligence so to speak it  could take you more or less time to  learn those skills and to play off of  the reactions of the person you're  reading but can you tell us a little bit  about how long your training was and  what it consisted of yes so I've always  really been good at reading people at  reading their expressions on their face  and and guessing what they're thinking  based on that and I never really took it  the next step obviously to to pretend to  be psychic so this was really really  easy for me I would say I read books for  about two months before I actually did  my first live show and and I would just  practice even online I would go on  online chats and even just seeing  somebody's picture and talking to them  online I was able to guess things like  their name by just putting a letter on  the keyboard and then they'll say oh  that's that letters in my name and then  I can kind of figure it out from there  and I've also used warm reading hot  reading where you can research the  person who's in front of you before they  get there or while they're there  there are a lot of different tricks that  I used I would say about two three  months of total preparation and I didn't  have any dissatisfied customers um what  are some of the key books in your  research that you read Oh  psychic sciences is a really good one  that's from the point from the point of  view of believers of course but then I  also read a lot of books by James Randi  he has really done so much work on that  topic that there wasn't there isn't  anybody who's done more than him so I  read all of his books on the topic yeah  and it helped me out so much because I  was able to see how he exposed other  psychics and so I was able to learn from  those mistakes and when people tried to  expose me which did happen quite a few  times I was able to anticipate that and  then make it even more impressive for  them really  so you said earlier that similarly to  religious beliefs that their it is  difficult to get people to critically  re-examine how they came to these sort  of supernatural beliefs can you give us  some of the better ways to try to get  people to re-examine those beliefs and  I'm sort of brought to the basic sort of  epistemology Socratic method of  questioning that we've heard from people  like Peter Bergen and others right  exploring how someone came to a  conclusion and what it might take to  change their mind are sort of the some  of the few inroads that we might be able  to make that's exactly right I talk a  lot in the book about the Socratic  method and how questions help people  understand these concepts more than  statements so if I were to tell you this  is how a psychic works people are less  likely to believe believe me than if I  said well how do you think they came to  this conclusion why would this psychic  say I'm feeling a letter and and and  then named the letter why would a ghost  or a spirit speak to them in letters and  fragments instead of if they're really  they're actually speaking to them and  and why is everything so vague and and  so when I asked those type of types of  questions and it's not like anybody is  like Oh psychics are fake and all this  stuff is fake and walked away but it  does plant that seed of doubt and and  that's really what it's all about I also  talked about how  do all that while being extremely nice  it never helps or it rarely helps to be  confrontational and so I'm very polite  in all my discussions and I really try  to focus on the actual issues to try to  criticize with kindness as Daniel  Dennett has said and and it seems to  work very well that way the book is  fascinating we've been speaking with the  author of the new book no sacred cows  David McAfee he's a religious Studies  graduate six-time published author and  also the founder of the party of Reason  and progress David great job with the  book and thanks so much for talking to  us about it  thank you very much for having me again  I appreciate it

